  "status": "published",
  "title": "Birdhouse Kit for Two",
  "summary": "Two birdhouses cut from one board. A good one to build with a kid on a Saturday morning.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 1,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Sized for house wrens. Mark the cedar board for two sets of parts, each a 8 1/2 in front, a 10 in back, two 8 1/2 in sides, a 4 in floor and a 7 1/2 in roof; that uses 94 in of the 96 in board. An adult makes the saw cuts; the kid holds the speed square and marks the lines.",
    "Clip 1/4 in off each corner of both floors for drainage, and drill two 1/4 in vent holes near the top of every side.",
    "On each front, drill a 1 in entrance hole centred 6 in above the bottom edge. Leave off any perch: it helps predators more than wrens.",
    "Glue and nail the back to one side, then the front to the same side, with the back's extra 1 1/2 in sticking up above the roof line. Fit the floor between them 1/4 in up from the bottom.",
    "Hang the second side as a clean-out door: drive one nail through the front and one through the back, 1 in below the top, as pivots, and one more near the bottom as a latch. Leave it unglued.",
    "Glue and nail the roof on so it overhangs the front, sand any splinters, and leave the cedar unfinished.",
    "Screw each box through the back to a post or tree 5 to 10 ft up. Wrens defend territory, so put the two boxes at least 100 ft apart."
  ],
